%[text] # Introduction to ODEs and Numerical Solvers
%%
%[text] ## Why Solve ODEs?
%[text] Ordinary differential equations are the standard way to model how physical systems **change over time**. They appear across engineering disciplines:
%[text] - Mechanical: vibration, vehicle dynamics, flight mechanics
%[text] - Electrical: circuit transients, control systems
%[text] - Naval: ship maneuvering, buoyancy response, heat transfer \
%[text] ![](text:image:7065)
%[text] ![](text:image:06ab)
%[text] For most real systems an exact (analytical) solution either doesn't exist or is impractical to obtain. Numerical methods give us approximate solutions we can actually compute.
%[text:table]
%[text] |  | Analytical | Numerical |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Accuracy | Exact | Approximate (controllable) |
%[text] | Applicability | Simple or special-case ODEs | Nearly any ODE |
%[text] | Output | Formula | Vectors of $(t, y)$ values |
%[text] | Insight | High — shows structure | Lower — just data points |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## A Physical Example — Newton's Law of Cooling
%[text] A cup of coffee starts at 90 °C in a 20 °C room. The rate of cooling is proportional to the temperature difference:
%[text] $\\frac{dy}{dt} = -k\\,(y - \\theta\_e), \\quad y(0) = 90$
%[text] where $y(t)$ is temperature (°C), $\\theta\_e = 20$ °C is room temperature, and $k = 0.01$ min$^{-1}$.
%[text] This ODE has a known exact solution, which makes it a good teaching example — we can check how well the numerical method performs.
%[text] $y(t) = \\theta\_e + (y\_0 - \\theta\_e)\\,e^{-kt}$
tt      = linspace(0, 200, 500);
y_exact = 20 + (90 - 20)*exp(-0.01*tt);
figure(1)
clf()
plot(tt, y_exact, 'k--', 'LineWidth', 2, 'DisplayName', 'Exact solution')
hold on
yline(20, 'Color', [0.6 0.6 0.6], 'LineStyle', ':', 'DisplayName', '\theta_e = 20 deg C')
xlabel('Time (min)')
ylabel('Temperature (deg C)')
grid on
legend()
%%
%[text] ## Euler's Method
%[text] Euler's method is the simplest numerical ODE solver. At each step it uses the current slope (the ODE) to estimate the next value:
%[text] $y\_{k+1} = y\_k + \\Delta t \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt}(t\_k,\\, y\_k)$
%[text] It is worth understanding what is happening "under the hood" before using a black-box solver.
%[text] **Step 0 — Initialize** at the initial condition:
t_euler = [0];
y_euler = [90];
dt = 20;
%[text] **Step 1 — Evaluate the rate of change** at the current state:
t    = t_euler(end);
y    = y_euler(end);
dydt = -0.01 * (y - 20)
%[text] **Step 2 — Step forward** using the slope:
t_euler(end+1) = t + dt;
y_euler(end+1) = y + dydt * dt;
plot(t_euler, y_euler, 'bo-', 'HandleVisibility', 'off')
%%
%[text] **Repeat** — same two steps from the new state:
t    = t_euler(end);
y    = y_euler(end);
dydt = -0.01 * (y - 20);
t_euler(end+1) = t + dt;
y_euler(end+1) = y + dydt * dt;
plot(t_euler, y_euler, 'bo-', 'HandleVisibility', 'off')
%%
%[text] Repeating by hand quickly becomes tedious — this is exactly what a loop is for.
while t_euler(end) < 200
    t    = t_euler(end);
    y    = y_euler(end);
    dydt = -0.01 * (y - 20);
    t_euler(end+1) = t + dt;
    y_euler(end+1) = y + dydt * dt;
end
plot(t_euler, y_euler, 'bo-', 'DisplayName', sprintf('Euler  \\Deltat = %d min', dt))
legend()
%%
%[text] ## Step Size and Accuracy
%[text] A smaller step size gives a more accurate solution at the cost of more computation. A practical rule of thumb: halve the step size until the solution stops changing significantly.
figure(2)
clf()
plot(tt, y_exact, 'k--', 'LineWidth', 2, 'DisplayName', 'Exact')
hold on
for dt = [40, 20, 5]
    te = [0]; ye = [90];
    while te(end) < 200
        dydt       = -0.01 * (ye(end) - 20);
        te(end+1)  = te(end) + dt;
        ye(end+1)  = ye(end) + dydt * dt;
    end
    plot(te, ye, 'DisplayName', sprintf('Euler  \\Deltat = %d min', dt))
end
xlabel('Time (min)'); ylabel('Temperature (deg C)'); legend(); grid on
%%
%[text] ## The Separation Principle
%[text] Hardcoding the ODE into the loop ties the solver to one specific problem. A better design keeps the **algorithm** (Euler) separate from the **model** (the ODE) by passing the rate function as a handle — the same pattern used with `fzero` and `fminbnd`.
%[text] Define the model as a local function:
function dydt = cooling_rate(t, y)
    % t is required by the solver interface even though this ODE does not
    % depend on time explicitly. ode45 always passes t as the first argument.
    k       = 0.01;
    theta_e = 20;
    dydt    = -k * (y - theta_e);
end
%[text] The solver loop now works for *any* ODE — swap the handle, nothing else changes:
rate_func = @cooling_rate;
tspan = [0, 200];
y0    = 90;
dt    = 20;

te = [tspan(1)]; ye = [y0];
while te(end) < tspan(2)
    dydt      = rate_func(te(end), ye(end));
    te(end+1) = te(end) + dt;
    ye(end+1) = ye(end) + dydt * dt;
end
%%
%[text] ## ode45 — MATLAB's Built-In Solver
%[text] `ode45` uses the same separation principle with a standard interface shared by all MATLAB ODE solvers:
%[text] `[t, y] = ode45(odefun, tspan, y0)`
%[text] - `odefun` — function handle; signature must be `dydt = f(t, y)`
%[text] - `tspan` — `[t_start, t_end]`
%[text] - `y0` — initial condition \
%[text] Unlike Euler, `ode45` automatically adjusts its step size to control error — taking small steps where the solution changes quickly and large steps where it is smooth.
[t_ode, y_ode] = ode45(@cooling_rate, tspan, y0);
%%
%[text] Compare all three on one plot:
figure(3)
clf()
plot(tt, y_exact, 'k--', 'LineWidth', 2, 'DisplayName', 'Exact')
hold on
plot(te, ye, 'r-o', 'LineWidth', 1.5, 'DisplayName', sprintf('Euler  \\Deltat = %d min', dt))
plot(t_ode, y_ode, 'b-', 'LineWidth', 1.5, 'DisplayName', 'ode45')
xlabel('Time (min)'); ylabel('Temperature (deg C)')
legend(); grid on
%[text] Notice that `ode45` closely tracks the exact solution while Euler accumulates error — especially early when the temperature is changing most rapidly.

